<div class="wrap column">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Ferrite Pocket privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">Ferrite Pocket doesn't collect, track or sell anything about you. It has no account, no
      analytics, no ads and no crash reporting of its own.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Your radio</h3>
    <p>Pocket talks to your own radio over Bluetooth. What it reads from the radio (channels, stations heard,
      messages, waypoints) stays on your phone.</p>

    <h3>Your position</h3>
    <p>Pocket uses your phone's position for the map, distances and satellite passes. While your radio has no GPS
      fix, Pocket gives it your position over Bluetooth. The radio uses it locally and never sends it out in a
      beacon. Pocket never sends your position to us or anyone else.</p>

    <h3>Nearby repeaters and airports</h3>
    <p>Pocket downloads the list for the area you're in from data.ferritefw.dev. The area is a 2-character grid
      square (about 2,000 by 1,000 km), so the request says only that much about where you are. The request carries
      no identifier for you or your phone, and we don't log or keep these requests, or tie them to anyone.</p>

    <h3>Satellite data</h3>
    <p>Pocket downloads the same satellite file for everyone, at most once a day.</p>

    <h3>Hosting</h3>
    <p>data.ferritefw.dev and ferritefw.dev are static sites hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub, not Ferrite, runs those
      servers, and GitHub logs and stores every visitor's IP address for security (see
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/about-github-pages#data-collection">About
      GitHub Pages</a>). We have no access to those logs.</p>

    <h3>Maps</h3>
    <p>The map is Apple Maps (Google Maps on Android), under Apple's (Google's) own privacy policy.</p>

    <h3>Sharing</h3>
    <p>A channel or station you share goes where you send it (Messages, Mail, AirDrop). A share link
      (<code>ferritefw.dev/s#...</code>) carries the channels after the <code>#</code>, which a browser never sends
      to the server. With Pocket installed, the link opens Pocket and no web page loads. In a browser, the page is
      counted like the rest of ferritefw.dev with <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com">GoatCounter</a>: no cookies,
      nothing personal kept, and only the page's address, never the channels.</p>

    <h3>Questions</h3>
    <p><a href="mailto:privacy@ferritefw.dev">privacy@ferritefw.dev</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
